 - **Found on:** [Portal](../../dm/games/01007BB017812000/README.md). No other profile's RomFS has a zip archive holding shaders.
 
+### NRO modules that keep their shaders in a zlib package
+
+- **Symptom:** a split over a game whose engine is `nn::ro` NRO modules finds GLSLC outputs in some of them and none in the launcher, while the game draws hundreds of programs the map lacks; a `DM_NVN_SHDUMP` of those programs holds control sections and code areas that a scan of every game file does not find.
+- **Cause:** the launcher ships its shaders beside the module, not in it: `launcher_dll/launcher.pkdswitch` is one zlib stream per entry, `158` of them indexed by `launcher_dll/launcher.pkiswitch` (`u32` count, then `u32 stored`, `offset`, `hash`, `raw` per entry). A deflated member hides the GLSLC magic from every scan of the file, as a zip member does ([GLSLC outputs in a zip archive](#glslc-outputs-in-a-zip-archive-and-those-nros-compile-at-run-time)). The 5 KB `main` of such a port only mounts the RomFS and loads the NRR, so it holds nothing either, and the launcher's own NRO can be free of programs while the engine it starts has them.
+- **Fix:** the chain has no reader for this package yet. One of the shape of `bnd2_resources` would do it: read the index, inflate each entry, scan it as a loose file — every output must still pass the control / code / length check. A profile registered before such a reader exists carries only what its own split writes, plus the rows a run-time dump reproduces ([Shaders whose bytes exist only in a run-time dump](#shaders-whose-bytes-exist-only-in-a-run-time-dump)); the dump's bytes themselves do not go in the map.
+- **Found on:** [LEGO Harry Potter Collection](../../dm/games/010052A00B5D2000/README.md) (update 1.0.2, SDK 5.4.0): `196` GLSLC outputs in `libharry1.nro` and `231` in `libharry2.nro` (372 distinct keys), none in the 741 KB `libHarryLauncher.nro`; the launcher's package holds 13 more, and 489 keys of the bring-up's 865-key map are in no file whose bytes a scan reads, the four `.DATNX` archives (12.9 GB, block-compressed) left undecoded.
+
 ### GLSLC outputs in Criterion's Bundle 2 resources
 
 - **Symptom:** an NVN game's split finds a few dozen shaders (`main`'s and a loose file's) and the screen stays black but for the loading indicator, with `[RED] shader … is not in the shader map` and `program has no vertex shader`; the RomFS holds `.BNDL` files that start with `bnd2`.
@@ -400,6 +407,13 @@ Finding, keying and translating shaders (`dm/gfx/tools/shader_split.py`, `dmsh_b
 - **Fix:** the keys are now computed inside `SetShaders` (`hle_nvn.c` `prog_key`). Mario calls `SetShaders` for some programs with a code address that is not mapped yet, so a stage whose code is not in a registered memory pool at that moment is hashed at its first draw, as before.
 - **Found on:** [Fire Emblem Engage](../../dm/games/0100A6301214E000/README.md) and Unity games in general (the source names no other); [Super Mario Bros. Wonder](../../dm/games/010015100B514000/README.md) (the unmapped code addresses).
 
+### Shaders whose bytes exist only in a run-time dump
+
+- **Symptom:** a split over a game whose engine assembles its programs itself finds only part of what the game draws — 372 of LEGO Harry Potter Collection's 865 programs — and a `DM_NVN_SHDUMP` of the rest holds control sections and code areas that no pass over the RomFS finds: not loose, not in the launcher's zlib package, not in its block-compressed archives, and the game never calls `glslcCompile` either, so there is no run-time GLSLC output to capture.
+- **Cause:** those programs are built in RAM by the engine (`…\SwitchFinalD11DLLStatic\…` paths in the NROs' code), so their bytes exist only in the run that made them. There is no container to find. A map row whose source no command reproduces would make the profile claim a coverage the repository cannot rebuild, and the bytes may not be committed ([CONTRIBUTING.md](../../CONTRIBUTING.md), "Never game files or keys"), so such a shader used to have no row at all, even though the user's own run had the bytes in hand.
+- **Fix:** the dump is a source of its own, and what is committed is the key and its provenance line. `dm/gfx/tools/dmsh_shdump.py <DM_NVN_SHDUMP dir> <package dir> <shader map>` reads the dump's `<key>.ctl` / `<key>.code` (the `SetShaders-` copy the host writes where it computes the key, else the draw-time one), recomputes each pair's key with the host's rule (`sha256(control[0:nctl] + code)[:16]`, `nctl` from control +0x20, the profile's `shader_key_skip` masked as `key_ctl` masks it) and **rejects** a pair whose sha256 is not the key in its file name — a dump of freed memory, a short write or a renamed file cannot put a row in the map. Stage is `glslc_stage`'s (the code area's header, headerless compute from control +0x748); the row's source is `/shdump/<key>.ctl#SHDUMP@0x0`, the shape every other row has with the dump's file name as the label; the package is `<stage>_<group>.bin` with the group the profile's `shader_groups` gives that label (`other` when the container is unknown, as Hot Pursuit's `SHADERS.BNDL` rows are), so a profile can name it (`/shdump/=shdump` → `ps_shdump.bin`). The tool appends each row at the next free record of the package file the split wrote, never rewrites it, adds nothing on a second run, and refuses a map whose header is not the split's or a package directory inside the repository. It runs after `shader_split.py`, which rewrites the map. A compute shader taken from a dump also needs its answer-table row (`shader_facts.tsv`); the tool prints the line.
+- **Found on:** [LEGO Harry Potter Collection](../../dm/games/010052A00B5D2000/README.md) (update 1.0.2): 493 rows (364 pixel, 129 vertex) from a dump of 924 pairs, which with the split's 372 make the 865-key map the first four years render through; the packages then hold 252 + 364 pixel and 120 + 129 vertex records, each hashing to the key its row names, and a second run adds nothing.
+
 ### Control sections shared among shaders that differ only in their compile information
 
 - **Symptom:** `[RED] shader … is not in the shader map` for shaders whose control section and code area are both in the game files, but in different records: dumped (`DM_NVN_SHDUMP`), the control section is one record's and the code area another's.
