Off by default, reads only (no draw is changed, skipped or clamped). Answers whether the
glyph wall's stale vertex stream is a host read-before-write.

DM_NVN_VBSCAN=<from>-<to> in guest frames: for every stride-28 stream of 32 or more
vertices it logs the bound address, count and first four words, hashes the stream and
searches the 48 MB around it for the clean frame's first glyph vertex, then reads the
probed addresses again at the end of the submission, at the present and at the next
submission's end. At each present it also classifies the four ring slots at the frame's
three stream offsets (0 / 4704 / 9408 = glyph run 1, run 2, the full-screen quad): Q the
quad, G a glyph run, Z zeros.

What it measured (logs/rb2.log, wall at present row 1286): the wall frame's two
168-vertex draws bind 0x383e62000 and 0x383e63260, which hold the full-screen quad
(w0=bf000000,bf000000,00000000,fe000000) and 4704 zero bytes; both are unchanged at the
submission's end and at the present, the glyph stream is nowhere in the ring, and the
slot is only built during the next frame. The GPU reads exactly these bytes: memory
pools are zero-copy buffers over the guest's own pages (mtl_pool_add, newBufferWithBytes-
