From 12422923a864482117404bbe3ab29d90ff15542b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thatSchloesser <sschloesser2@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 15:49:52 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 07/32] fix(nvn): freeze the vertex bytes a draw fetches at
 draw time

bind_vbufs binds the guest address itself: memory pools are zero-copy buffers over the
guest's own pages and nothing separates a draw from the CPU writes of the frame after
it. A console's GPU runs behind its CPU; here it starts about 0.2 ms after the commit,
so a draw can read a stream the guest is still filling -- a tear, and a tear in a
position attribute is a vertex at infinity, a beam or a spike across the frame -- or a
ring slot the guest has not filled yet, which is the previous screen's content.

A draw now copies the bytes it fetches into the submission arena before its render
encoder, as ubd_shadow does for the uniform range it reads (vbuf_unaligned, fresh per
draw: a ring filled and drawn twice inside one submission must not reuse the earlier
copy). The cost is bounded by class: <= 16 KB always, <= 256 KB while the submission's
24 MB budget lasts, larger streams (static meshes) never. DM_NVN_VB_SNAP=0 restores the
old path.

Measured on LEGO Harry Potter Years 5-7 (010052A00B5D2000), 95 s frame-indexed runs:
an unbounded first cut copied 1100 streams / 724 MB a frame and reached 12 GB resident
(killed by the memory watchdog); this one copies 295-680 streams / 24-25 MB a frame,
resident +130 MB, frame time p50 16.68 ms unchanged (GPU-bound), submit+present
4.11 -> 4.90 ms, HUD text crisp in 11 sampled gameplay frames.

It is not a fix for that title's screen-change artefacts: the 65.0-luma one is
unchanged (65.01 -> 65.11) and the glyph wall did not reproduce in these runs. The
previous session's VBSCAN found the bytes identical at draw time, at submission end and
at present, so a copy taken at draw time freezes exactly the stale bytes the GPU would
have read.
