From 74f223822effd9512029f6c4750019f1ad29c46a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thatSchloesser <sschloesser2@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:11:31 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 08/32] fix(nvn): freeze the index buffer too, and run the
 pipeline a frame behind the CPU

Two more places where the GPU reads guest memory that the CPU is still writing.

Index buffer: an indexed draw bound the guest address zero-copy, exactly as
bind_vbufs did for vertex streams, and nothing separated it from the CPU writes of
the next frame. A torn INDEX buffer is not a torn position: every index still names a
vertex that exists, so the shader outputs a sane position and no clamp on its output
can see the damage -- but a triangle can join two unrelated vertices and become a
sliver across the frame with the mesh's colours interpolated along it. That is the
beams / rainbow fans of a torn dynamic mesh. The indices a draw reads are now copied
into the submission arena at draw time (capped 512 KB a draw, 32 MB a submission).
DM_NVN_IB_SNAP=0 restores the old binding.

Pipeline pacing (on by default, DM_NVN_PIPE_LAG=0 to disable): a frame's GPU work is
committed when a further submission has begun recording, instead of at its own
present. The machinery already existed as DM_NVN_SUBMIT_LAG; what changed is the
default and the wording. Measured over 2100 frames of the Years 5-7 front end:
held 2100, released by a later submit 2093, by a guest wait 0, by a present 0, by the
50 ms deadlock bound 6; frame time p50 16.68 ms, p95 17.00 ms (unchanged).

Both are measured with the per-present full-frame luma series, not screenshots: the
snapshots do not move that series (155 vs 190 isolated bright rows over 150 s, i.e.
noise), which is why it is reported as a proxy that does not resolve this artefact
and not as evidence that the beams are gone.
