From 6649a2fab1495142f7b60ed86df507af3a92ae78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: orchestrator <orch@local>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:11:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 14/32] diag(nvn): DM_NVN_FULLSYNC, DM_NVN_SYNCLOG,
 DM_NVN_DSPROBE and DM_NVN_QRDTECT -- what the serialisation experiment, the
 guest's own waits and the quad-stream check showed

DM_NVN_FULLSYNC=<0|1|2>: 0 (default) is today's behaviour exactly; 1 waits for the submission's own fence
before the guest continues, 2 for the previous submission's. Measured on LEGO HP Years 5-7: full
serialisation does NOT remove the one-frame artifacts (A 6.66, B 3.68 events per 100 gameplay presents
against a baseline of 6.59-7.03 / 3.45-3.77), so the artifacts are not a CPU/GPU data hazard on guest
memory even though the vertex streams bind the guest address itself.

DM_NVN_SYNCLOG=1: one line per guest nvnSyncWait (fence, newest, done, behind, timeout, blocked ms,
result). Measured: the game does wait, always on the fence one submission back with an infinite timeout,
3,458 times over 8,000 submissions, and the host never has it already complete at entry -- the host is
not answering early.

DM_NVN_DSPROBE=1: DM_NVN_DRAWSTATE's per-draw reads without the MLOG line, to separate the state it
reads from the time it costs.

DM_NVN_QRDTECT=<vs key>: a passive check (one strcmp per draw, a 16-byte compare on the small draws of
that shader) for a small draw reading the full-screen quad's stream (-0.5,-0.5,0,-2.0). Unlike
DM_NVN_DRAWSTATE it does not suppress the artifact: 20,000+ small glyph draws checked over 7,538
gameplay presents that carried 498 A episodes, 0 of them read the quad's stream.

mtl_done_fence(): g_done for diagnostics.
