Subject: [PATCH 18/32] diag(nvn): DM_NVN_RINGTRACE per-slot page watch --
 store runs, frame chains and the staging array, so the block a UI draw read
 is attributed to code, thread and time

The old arming put six watches on six addresses of one frame's arena, which the drifting cursor never
revisits (1 of 3,557 draws bound one). This arms one watch per 8 MB ring slot on the 16 KB page the slot's
UI blocks live in, keeps every store into it as a contiguous run (probe.c: dm_watch_dyn_runs, 12 words per
run: ea0, ea1, t0, t1, pc, v0, tid and the first four guest frame-chain entries), and prints/clears the
window when the slot comes round again three frames later, so the fill of the block this frame's draw names
is inside it.

Measured with it: the only writer of the pool's UI page is nro0's array upload (0x1bc9b0, called at
0x1bc918 from 0x1bc8a0 from 0x15f498), T=1001, memcpy at sdk+0x489a78, chain nro0+0x1bca68<-nro0+0x1bc91c,
~63,744 B per frame in 400-store bursts; and DM_NVN_RINGTRACE_ARRAY/ARRDUMP show the pool block a draw
reads holding the staging array's bytes from the previous emission, byte for byte, at the same offset
(50 of 50 sampled draws, runs/pw7).

Retargeting a live range is deliberately NOT done: the fault handler resolves the alias against the live
page fields, so moving a range that races a store in flight writes it through the wrong alias (measured:
SIGBUS in guest code, runs/pw9); the trace reports the moved page instead. Off by default: with
DM_NVN_RINGTRACE unset, rt_live() is false and nothing in this block runs.
